"An entity relationship diagram models domain entities and the cardinality and optionality of their relationships.\n\nUse when: Agree the meaning and relationships of stored domain information. Review relationship constraints before selecting a physical schema.\n\nAvoid when: Deployment paths or object behavior is the central question.\n\nVisual grammar: Entity boxes contain concise names and relevant attributes. Relationship lines carry domain verbs and end cardinalities. A notation key explains crow’s foot or Chen symbols, including optional participation.\n\nBuild: Choose conceptual or logical scope and one notation. Identify entities by stable domain identities. Add relationships and read each end aloud with its cardinality. Arrange related entities in clusters and route relationship lines outside attribute compartments.\n\nCheck: Both ends state minimum and maximum participation where known. Many-to-many relationships have intentional logical meaning. Attribute and entity names use consistent domain vocabulary. Cardinality symbols and labels do not collide with entity borders.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing optional relationships with nullable attributes. Adding implementation foreign keys before resolving entity meaning.\n\nExample: Customer places zero or more Orders; each Order belongs to exactly one Customer and contains one or more Lines.\n\nAlternatives: Relational schema diagram, Class diagram."
